\paragraph{NLP finetuning control.}\looseness=-1
To test whether exit collapse is specific to action-oriented training, we probe three chat-finetuned models that share backbones with our VLAs but lack action heads or robot data: Llama-2-7b-chat (OpenVLA), Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct (TraceVLA), and Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct (NORA).\footnote{Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct shares the Qwen2.5 language backbone with NORA's Qwen2.5-VL-3B but lacks the vision encoder and cross-attention modules; see Appendix~\ref{sec:nlp_control_appendix}.}
All three produce flat $R^2$ profiles ($\Delta R^2 = 0.000$; $R^2 = 0.155 \pm 0.001$, $0.155 \pm 0.000$, and $0.155 \pm 0.000$ respectively; Appendix~\ref{sec:nlp_control_appendix}, Figure~\ref{fig:nlp_control_r2}), contrasting with VLM-to-VLA collapse ($\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.455$).
The absence of exit collapse in any NLP control is inconsistent with exit collapse being a generic consequence of language-model fine-tuning.
\textbf{In sum, exit collapse is consistently associated with VLM-to-VLA transitions and absent in both VLA-to-VLA and NLP-only fine-tuning.}

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{NLP finetuning control.}\looseness=-1
To test whether exit collapse is specific to action-oriented training, we probe three chat-finetuned models sharing backbones with our VLAs but lacking action heads or robot data: Llama-2-7b-chat, Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct, and Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct.\footnote{Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct shares the Qwen2.5 language backbone with NORA's Qwen2.5-VL-3B but lacks the vision encoder; see Appendix~\ref{sec:nlp_control_appendix}.}
All three produce flat $R^2$ profiles ($\Delta R^2 = 0.000$; Figure~\ref{fig:nlp_control_r2}), contrasting with VLM-to-VLA collapse ($\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.455$).
\textbf{Exit collapse is consistently associated with VLM-to-VLA transitions and absent in both VLA-to-VLA and NLP-only fine-tuning.}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_nlp_control_r2.pdf}
\vspace{-6pt}
\caption{\textbf{NLP controls produce flat $R^2$ profiles.} Each panel overlays the VLA trained (blue) and reference (orange) per-layer action-probe $R^2$ with the NLP control (dashed gray, $R^2 = 0.155$). VLM-to-VLA transitions exhibit peak-to-exit collapse; NLP controls remain flat ($\Delta R^2 = 0.000$).}
\label{fig:nlp_control_r2}
\vspace{-6pt}
\end{figure}

Matched-size ablation reveals that instruction sensitivity is distributed across heads in proportion to their instruction attention fraction ($d = 0.65$ vs.\ $0.60$, 8\% gap; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:specificity_control}), consistent with instruction information being woven into the residual stream rather than concentrated in a dedicated circuit.
Dose-response ablation of L1 instruction-dominant heads (Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}) yields monotonic trajectory divergence ($d = 0.48$--$0.64$), with steep rise through 12 heads and diminishing returns beyond 20, providing causal evidence that L1 gating contributes to downstream action computation.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/fig_dose_response.pdf}
\vspace{-6pt}
\caption{Dose-response: trajectory divergence (Cohen's $d$) vs.\ number of ablated L1 instruction-dominant heads. Error bars: 95\% bootstrap CIs. Dashed red: control ablation (8 non-instruction heads). Steep rise through 12 heads, plateau at 16--20, diminishing returns beyond.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\vspace{-6pt}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\looseness=-1
We apply all three analyses (\S\ref{sec:cross_arch_analyses}) to the $3 \times 4$ architecture-suite matrix (10 of 12 cells filled; Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}).
For trained models, counterfactual sensitivity uses Cohen's $d$ on action MSE; reference VLMs use cosine distance on hidden representations (different scale; \S\ref{sec:cf_sensitivity}).
OpenVLA's OXE-pretrained checkpoint produces actions, permitting same-metric comparison (Bonferroni correction in Appendix~\ref{sec:bonferroni_correction}).

